description:
Decode a TCP header with its options, name its flags, and compute the checksum TCP and UDP use (lesson 4.03).

```cpp
struct Tcp
{
    std::uint16_t sourcePort;
    std::uint16_t destinationPort;
    std::uint32_t sequence;
    std::uint32_t acknowledgement;
    std::uint8_t headerLength;   // in bytes
    std::uint8_t flags;
    std::uint16_t window;
    std::optional<std::uint16_t> mss;
    std::optional<std::uint8_t> windowScale;
    bool sackPermitted;
};

std::optional<Tcp> parseTcp(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& segment);
std::string flagNames(std::uint8_t flags);
std::uint16_t transportChecksum(std::uint32_t source, std::uint32_t destination, std::uint8_t protocol,
                                const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& segment);
```

- **parseTcp** fails if the segment is shorter than 20 bytes, or the header length (data offset × 4) is under 20 or longer than the segment. It then walks the options between byte 20 and the end of the header: kind 0 ends the list, kind 1 is a single padding byte, and any other kind has a length byte that counts the kind and length bytes too. A length under 2, or an option that would run past the header, makes the parse fail. Record MSS (kind 2, length 4), window scale (kind 3, length 3) and SACK permitted (kind 4, length 2); skip other kinds.
- **flagNames** lists the set flags from bit 0 upwards, separated by commas: `FIN, SYN, RST, PSH, ACK, URG, ECE, CWR`. So `0x12` is `"SYN,ACK"` and no flags is `""`.
- **transportChecksum** runs the Internet checksum over the IPv4 pseudo-header (source, destination, a zero byte, the protocol, and the segment's length as 16 bits) followed by the segment, padding an odd last byte with zero. With the segment's checksum field set to zero it gives the value to write there; over a received segment it gives 0 when the checksum is right.

